<h2 id="short-answer">The short answer</h2>
<p>Start with one task your team does often, that follows clear rules, that works on information already in digital form and that does little harm when something goes wrong. Map how it runs today, automate the steps a machine can do reliably, keep a person on the decisions that need judgment, and measure the result before you move on to the next one.</p>

<h2 id="good-first-candidates">What makes a good first candidate?</h2>
<p>The best first automation is rarely the most impressive one. It is the one most likely to work and easiest to check. Look for a task that is:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Frequent.</strong> Done daily or weekly, so the time saved adds up and problems show quickly.</li>
  <li><strong>Rule-based.</strong> Someone could write the steps down and a new hire could follow them.</li>
  <li><strong>Already digital.</strong> The inputs arrive as email, forms, spreadsheets or records in a system, not on paper.</li>
  <li><strong>Low-risk.</strong> A mistake is caught before it reaches a customer or moves money.</li>
  <li><strong>Annoying.</strong> The team will be glad to hand it over, which helps when you ask them to test it.</li>
</ul>
<p>Re-keying data from one system into another, sending routine reminders, filing documents and building a weekly report from the same sources are typical examples.</p>

<h2 id="what-to-leave-for-later">What should you leave for later?</h2>
<p>Leave tasks that change every time, that depend on judgment nobody has written down, or where an error is costly or public. Pricing decisions, sensitive customer replies and anything that approves payments can be automated in part, but they are poor places to learn. Also leave any process the team disagrees about. Automating it only makes the disagreement faster.</p>

<h2 id="step-by-step">A step-by-step way to start</h2>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Map the current steps.</strong> Write down who does what, in which system, and where work waits. Watch the task being done once rather than relying on the official version.</li>
  <li><strong>Mark the predictable steps.</strong> Separate the steps that follow fixed rules from the ones that need a person to decide.</li>
  <li><strong>Decide where a person stays in the loop.</strong> Approvals, exceptions and anything sent to a customer usually need a human check, at least at first.</li>
  <li><strong>Choose the simplest tool that will do.</strong> Check the automation features of the software you already pay for, then an integration tool that connects your systems, and only then custom code.</li>
  <li><strong>Test it end to end with real cases.</strong> Include the awkward ones: the missing field, the duplicate, the late reply. Write a short runbook for when it fails.</li>
  <li><strong>Measure before and after.</strong> Track the time the task takes, how often it goes wrong and how long work waits. Without a before figure you will not know whether it worked.</li>
</ol>

<h2 id="where-ai-fits">Where does AI fit?</h2>
<p>Classic automation is good at moving structured data along fixed rules. AI helps with the steps that involve reading or writing: pulling the details out of an email or a PDF, sorting requests by type, or drafting a reply for someone to check. Those are also the steps where AI can be confidently wrong, so they are the ones to keep a person on until you trust the results.</p>
<p>Sometimes the right answer is no AI at all. A simpler rule or a change in process can solve the problem more cheaply and more reliably.</p>

<h2 id="after-the-first">What comes after the first one?</h2>
<p>Once the first automation runs reliably for a few weeks, look at what it taught you: where the data was messier than expected, which exceptions came up and how the team felt about the change. Then pick the next task with the same checklist. Small, proven steps build the trust that larger projects need.</p>
